      "writtenOn": "2019-02-28T02:32:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Explanation round 2:\n* We\u0027re compiling with Thumb instructions (Thumb 1 is 16-bits, Thumb-2 32-bits): \n\nhttp://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic\u003d/com.arm.doc.ddi0210c/CACBCAAE.html\n\nhttp://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic\u003d/com.arm.doc.dui0471i/CHDFEDDB.html\n\n* There is an LDR  pseudoinstruction in the code so we need to load that constant from somewhere (pseudoinstruction \"ldr SOFTWARE_PANIC_REASON_REG, \u003dPANIC_SW_DIV_ZERO\" and real generated instruction at address b8 in the disassembly): \n\nhttp://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic\u003d/com.arm.doc.dui0041c/Babbfdih.html\n\n* Although our architecture (armv7-E) appears to support hardware unaligned access, it\u0027s slower than aligned access:\n\nhttp://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic\u003d/com.arm.doc.faqs/ka15414.html\n\n* The last instruction in the assembly file is the branch (\"bl exception_panic\"). (It\u0027s a 32-bit Thumb2 instruction).\n\n* After the last instruction, we are at address 0xbe. This is not 32-bit aligned (because there are some Thumb1 16-bit instructions used). Instead of putting the constant for the LDR at an unaligned address, the compiler/assembler inserts padding.\n\n* In the case of gcc, it pads with 16-bits of zeros. In the case of clang, it pads with a 16-bit noop instruction. Either should be fine given that it should never be executed (\"exception_panic\" has the \"noreturn\" attribute).",
